# KJV Bible and Voice Reader Integration

## Context

The old React implementation is staged under `integrate/`; every initially
staged file is byte-identical to `/home/renniem/Workspace/thewriterco` commit
`fbdd6bf`. Missing feature dependencies and the page artwork are preserved in
`integrate/legacy_dependencies/README.md`.

The replacement must preserve the old tool's useful behavior without importing
Redux, Radix Themes, Tailwind, Framer Motion, Embla, or the old page shell. It
must use Skaia's React Router, Jotai state, shared UI primitives, dialog
patterns, theme context, and token-only CSS.

The corpus source is `/home/renniem/Workspace/kjv-bible` commit
`88723a44bb3e3f229a34f9cf11ce1b7acf971eee`.
Its 2026-07-30 audit records 66 books, 1,189 chapters, and 31,102 verses with
corpus fingerprint
`7c2eff0219d59c683b1d12739a64facb22807770e05daf20cf1a4d22ef1b739d5ec03268abb8c3201fd69eb1014cc45a37697cb8abaceccd316c2e473db0b264`.
Its `kjv.json` records the 1611 first publication, 1769 general editorial
basis, maintained repository origin, and a 2026-07-30 verse-level verification
reference to Bible SuperSearch KJV module 6.2.0. That reference is not an
independent bibliographical certification or a claim of vendor origin.

## Goal

- Provide a public `/kjv` Bible browser with the legacy book picker, search,
  reader, favorites, download, keyboard navigation, and browser voice controls.
- Support legacy query links such as
  `/kjv?bt=Matthew&c=6&v=25&o=1`.
- Support path links as
  `/kjv/:book/:chapter/:verse/:readerState`, where the canonical state is
  `open` or `closed` (for example `/kjv/matthew/6/25/open`).
- Serve a verified, internal KJV copy from Skaia's Go backend with no runtime
  GitHub dependency.
- Match Skaia's responsive, accessible, light/dark UI contracts while
  preserving the old workflow rather than its library-specific markup.

## Non-Goals

- Do not add alternate translations, commentary, AI study features, accounts
  synchronization, or server-side favorites.
- Do not add Redux, a new UI library, a carousel package, or a database
  migration.
- Do not claim vendor origin or stronger textual provenance than the corpus
  source documents.
- Do not make the existing realtime voice-chat subsystem own browser
  `SpeechSynthesis`; Bible narration is a separate local frontend capability.

## Contracts

### Backend

- Add `backend/internal/bible` using the normal handler/service boundary and an
  embedded read-only corpus store; place the exact 66 JSON files and integrity
  metadata inside that package so the Go binary is self-contained.
- The backend owns canonical titles, URL slugs, legacy division order,
  testament/division labels, chapter counts, verse counts, and title aliases.
  Use `Psalms`, not the stale legacy `Psalm`.
- Mount public, read-only endpoints:
  - `GET /api/bible/kjv/books`
  - `GET /api/bible/kjv/books/{book}`
- The list response returns translation metadata plus ordered book summaries.
  The book response returns `translation`, canonical `title`, `slug`, and the
  chapter/verse object plus the aligned marker DTO. Unknown books return `404`;
  malformed embedded text, metadata, or markers prevent service startup/tests
  from passing.
- Preserve the repository-wide API no-store policy. Client code may retain
  already loaded books in a bounded in-memory map for the current session.
- Record the source commit, text and rendering fingerprints, translation
  history, repository origin, and provider-verification caveat beside the
  embedded corpus.

### Frontend state and URLs

- Add a focused Bible atom module. Persist only preferences and user state:
  last selection, reader open state, favorites, speech-enabled preference, and
  selected `voiceURI`. Do not persist corpus JSON or transient
  speaking/paused/loading/error state.
- Parse query and path forms through one typed `BibleLocation` codec. On first
  load a valid URL is authoritative over remembered state; intentional UI
  navigation updates the same URL form, while narration auto-advance uses
  history replacement so it does not flood browser history.
- Accept `o=1|0` in query form and `open|closed` in path form. Canonicalize book
  aliases/case to backend titles/slugs. Validate chapter and verse after the
  book loads; show an inline recoverable not-found/error state instead of
  silently presenting the wrong scripture.
- Add both `/kjv` route shapes as public lazy routes, add Bible navigation in
  the shared header/footer, and add `/kjv` to the sitemap.

### Viewer and reader

- Translate the legacy divisions, search, book tiles, optional cover/page-flip
  animation, loading state, picker dialog, chapter/verse selectors, current
  verse, four following shadow verses, previous/next, next-five, favorite,
  favorites manager, and JSON download.
- Render marker sidecars without changing clean verse text: paragraph starts
  break the context flow, added words are italicized, and words of Christ use a
  theme-aware global red-letter token. Interpret offsets as zero-based Unicode
  code points with exclusive ends.
- Use the local staged `Bible_Page_Art.jpg`; no runtime GitHub image or scripture
  fetches. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Use Skaia `Button`, `Select`, `SearchField`, `.card`/`.section`, existing
  dialog styles, Lucide icons, and global CSS tokens. New component CSS owns
  layout/animation only and contains no hardcoded colors, token fallbacks, or
  native dialogs.
- Keep the reader route-scoped to the Bible page instead of mounting a global
  fixed panel on every Skaia page. The collapsed control remains usable without
  obscuring the site header/footer; the open reader is responsive and
  keyboard/focus accessible.

### Browser narration

- Feature-detect `speechSynthesis` and `SpeechSynthesisUtterance`; render an
  explanatory disabled state when unavailable.
- Populate voices initially and on `voiceschanged`. Restore a saved `voiceURI`
  only when present; otherwise prefer a browser-default English voice, then any
  English voice, then the first available voice.
- Implement real play, pause, resume, and stop behavior. Narrate one verse per
  utterance, advance on a successful end, continue across chapters within the
  selected book, and stop cleanly at the end of the book.
- Cancel and invalidate the active narration session on stop, book/route
  change, reader close, error, or unmount so stale callbacks cannot advance the
  verse. Changing voices while playing restarts only the current verse.
- Preserve shortcuts while the reader is open: Left/Right navigate, `V`
  toggles speech controls, Space plays/pauses, and Escape stops. Never intercept
  shortcuts from inputs, textareas, selects, buttons, or editable content.

## Steps

1. Import and verify the KJV corpus, add embedded corpus metadata/store/service,
   implement the two public handlers, mount them in `backend/main.go`, and add
   corpus, service, handler, route, and error tests.
2. Add typed frontend Bible API models/client, the URL codec, Jotai
   preference/favorites state, bounded book cache, and unit tests for aliases,
   query/path round trips, invalid coordinates, and persistence.
3. Translate the Bible browser and book-picker UI using Skaia primitives and
   CSS tokens; preserve legacy division/search/animation behavior and the local
   artwork without old UI/runtime dependencies.
4. Translate the reader, selectors, navigation, favorites, download, and
   responsive open/collapsed panel; connect every intentional state change to
   URL synchronization.
5. Implement and test the browser speech engine, asynchronous voice discovery,
   controls, verse/chapter progression, cancellation guards, unsupported
   browser state, and keyboard exclusions.
6. Wire public routes and shared navigation/sitemap, add page-level integration
   tests for both URL forms, verify responsive/light/dark/reduced-motion and
   keyboard/screen-reader behavior, then update affected specs and this tip.

## Verification

- Corpus source before import:
  `python script.py audit` and `sha512sum -c SHA512SUMS` in
  `/home/renniem/Workspace/kjv-bible`.
- Backend: `go test ./...` from `backend/`; tests must assert the 66/1,189/31,102
  totals, tracked fingerprint, canonical `Psalms`, endpoint ordering, known
  `Matthew 6:25`, and `404` behavior.
- Frontend: targeted Vitest suites plus `npm test -- --run`,
  `npm run lint`, `npm run check:native-dialogs`, and `npm run build` from
  `frontend/`.
- Manual URLs:
  `/kjv`, `/kjv?bt=Matthew&c=6&v=25&o=1`,
  `/kjv/matthew/6/25/open`, a multiword/numbered book, closed state, invalid
  book/chapter/verse, browser back/forward, and a direct hard refresh.
- Manual speech: delayed `voiceschanged`, missing saved voice, play/pause/resume,
  stop, voice change, verse/chapter boundary, end-of-book, navigation away, and
  browsers without Web Speech support.
- CSS drift:
  `rg "#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}|rgba\\(|rgb\\(|var\\([^)]*," frontend/src --glob '*.css'`.
- Dependency/runtime drift:
  `rg "@radix-ui/themes|tailwind|react-redux|redux|raw.githubusercontent.com|github.com/renniemaharaj/Resources" frontend/src backend/internal/bible`.

